# Fixed 2026-10-02, defect 130's class, lane recovery-b6 (the audit's group
# (e), row H-009, and the recovery instrument's `line-comment`). Symptom: a
# comment written as C, Java, JS, Rust, Go or Swift write it, `//`, was read
# as code: two `/` operators and whatever its prose held, so a backquote
# cost a stray character, a `(` an `unclosed_bracket`, an apostrophe an
# `unterminated_char`, and the line `expected_declaration` or
# `expected_expression` besides, up to four messages for one slip, and a
# `//` line at column 0 inside a body ended the body. Cause: the lexer knew
# one comment, `#`. It now reads `//` and a `/* … */` closed on its line as
# the comment they are, layout-neutral as a `#` line, nothing in them lexed,
# and tells each once (`comment_habit.hero`): the fix writes `#`, certain
# where the comment opens its line and a guess after code, where Python's
# integer division is a reading too; inside a bracket closed later on its
# line, and in an interpolation hole, `//` is read as that division, the
# `/` this language writes, and a quote the line never closes is prose's.

// a file's first comment, with a `(` and a don't in its prose  #~ unexpected_character

/// a doc comment above a function  #~ unexpected_character
function half(n: i64) -> i64
    return n / 2

function main()
    x = 1  // the count so far (see above)  #~ unexpected_character
    // a note on a line of its own  #~ unexpected_character
// at column 0, inside the body  #~ unexpected_character
    if x > 0  // always  #~ unexpected_character
        print(x)
    y = half(  // the argument  #~ unexpected_character
        x)
    print(y // 2)  #~ unexpected_character
    print(f"{y // 2}")  #~ unexpected_character
    z = 3  /* a block comment */  #~ unexpected_character
    /* one of its own line */  #~ unexpected_character
    w /* inside the line */ = 4  #~ unexpected_character
    print(z + w)

    # Inside a bracket closed on a later line, an apostrophe in the prose.
    xs: [  // the list's type  #~ unexpected_character
        i64] = [1]
    print(xs.len())
